1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
The first section in the musculoskeletal pathology chapter

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,640
deals with the skeletal system.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,040
The first disorder is achondroplasia.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:07,940
A means without.

5
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:10,920
Chondro refers to the chondrocytes that make cartilage,

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,640
and plasia refers to growth.

7
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,620
So this is a disorder of cartilage proliferation

8
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,100
in the growth plate.

9
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:19,340
It's due to an activating mutation

10
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:21,420
in fibroblast growth factor receptor 3.

11
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,180
Very high yield board examiners like to go after this.

12
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,740
You need to know that it is an activating mutation,

13
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:29,060
and you need to know that the mutation

14
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:31,300
is in fibroblast growth factor receptor 3.

15
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,640
Now what happens here is that you get this receptor turned on

16
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:35,580
because of the mutation,

17
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,500
and when you turn the receptor on,

18
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:40,180
that inhibits the growth of the cartilage.

19
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:42,000
This is autosomal dominant.

20
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:45,280
The mutations are related to increased paternal age,

21
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,720
and most mutations are sporadic.

22
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:50,660
The result of achondroplasia will be that the patients

23
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:54,120
will have short extremities with normal-sized head and chest.

24
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,900
This is very high yield, and it goes back to embryology.

25
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,080
Let's remind ourselves that there are two ways

26
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,340
by which bone can be formed.

27
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:03,020
The first is called intramembranous,

28
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:05,800
and in intramembranous, you simply produce bone

29
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,000
from a connective tissue matrix.

30
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:09,500
So you've got connective tissue,

31
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:11,180
and the connective tissue becomes bone,

32
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:14,660
and that's the way by which the bones of the skull are made,

33
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,500
and the bones of the chest are made,

34
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,180
and the flat bones, for example, of the wrist are made.

35
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,620
Again, this is referred to as intramembranous bone formation.

36
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,240
Now, in this particular disorder,

37
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,900
there is no problem with intramembranous bone formation,

38
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,500
and so they have a normal-sized head

39
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:29,760
and a normal-sized chest.

40
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,900
The second way by which bone is formed

41
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,920
is called endochondral bone formation,

42
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,900
and this is where you establish a cartilage matrix,

43
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:41,780
the cartilage dies, it gets calcified and mineralized,

44
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:42,880
and then becomes bone.

45
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,260
Again, that's called endochondral bone formation,

46
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:48,580
and that's the key mechanism by which our long bones grow.

47
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:50,980
You're well aware of the histology of the growth plate.

48
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:52,300
Of course, it's made of cartilage,

49
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:54,740
and as the growth plate expands,

50
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:58,540
the chondrocytes begin to die at one edge of that plate,

51
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:00,280
and then they're replaced with bone,

52
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:02,040
and that's how the long bones get longer.

53
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:03,880
And so these patients are going to present

54
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:05,960
with short extremities because they have a defect

55
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,300
in their proliferation of chondrocytes

56
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,200
at the cartilage growth plate.

57
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,860
Now, important to note that mental function will be fine,

58
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:13,980
lifespan will be fine,

59
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:16,080
and fertility will also not be affected.

60
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:19,220
The next disorder is osteogenesis imperfecta.

61
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:21,240
This is imperfect formation of bone.

62
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:23,560
It's a congenital defect of bone formation,

63
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:24,900
and it results in weak bone.

64
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:28,240
It's most commonly due to an autosomal dominant defect

65
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:29,280
in type 1 collagen.

66
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:32,640
Remember that type 1 collagen is the collagen

67
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:34,000
that is present in bone,

68
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:35,500
and some people remember that

69
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,040
by just remembering that the word bone

70
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,100
has the letter 1 in it.

71
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,740
The clinical features are going to be multiple fractures.

72
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:41,900
Without type 1 collagen,

73
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:43,280
the bone will be very weak,

74
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:44,040
and it'll fracture.

75
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,720
In addition, the patients will have blue sclera.

76
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,500
The idea here is that the sclera,

77
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,160
which is the white of the eye,

78
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,900
normally contains a lot of type 1 collagen.

79
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,160
And so if there's a defect in type 1 collagen,

80
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,480
the sclera will be thin,

81
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:57,900
and you'll see blue sclera.

82
00:02:58,260 --> 00:02:59,760
Now, board examiners like to ask you

83
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:01,020
why the sclera are blue,

84
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:03,380
and the answer is that it's due to exposure

85
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:06,200
of the choroidal veins, high yield.

86
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,780
Exposure of the choroidal veins.

87
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:11,100
In addition, the patient can develop hearing loss,

88
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,400
and that's because you have little tiny bones

89
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,280
in the middle ear that help to transmit the sound

90
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:17,580
that comes from the outside world,

91
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,400
and those bones can then fracture

92
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,420
because they also need type 1 collagen.

93
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,600
And so again, hearing loss is an important feature as well.

94
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:27,060
Osteopetrosis is a disorder

95
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,540
in which you have abnormally thick, heavy bone.

96
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:31,160
Petrosis means rock-like.

97
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:33,780
And this arises with an inherited defect

98
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:34,800
of bone resorption.

99
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:36,540
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves

100
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,840
that bone formation is a balance

101
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:41,220
between the osteoblast, which lay down bone,

102
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:43,600
and the osteoclast, which resorb bone.

103
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,520
And this balance is constantly occurring throughout life

104
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:48,360
so that you're always laying down some bone

105
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:49,740
and you're always resorbing bone.

106
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:52,360
Now, imagine if you had a defect in bone resorption

107
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,280
so that you laid down bone, laid down bone,

108
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:56,340
laid down bone, but you couldn't resorb it.

109
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:57,660
If you can't resorb it,

110
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,080
that's going to result in abnormally thick, heavy bone.

111
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:01,660
Now, the interesting thing is that

112
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,700
despite this abnormally thick, heavy bone,

113
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:05,500
it actually fractures easily.

114
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:06,960
It's sort of like a piece of chalk.

115
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,020
It's thick, but you can easily crack it.

116
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:12,020
This disorder is due to poor osteoclast function.

117
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:14,860
Of course, the osteoclast are the cells that resorb bone,

118
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,980
and there are multiple genetic variants.

119
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,240
Of those, the carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation

120
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,600
is particularly high yield.

121
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:23,340
Examiners like to go after this

122
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,180
because it ties into physiology.

123
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:27,020
In order to understand this,

124
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,980
you need to know that an acidic environment

125
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:32,180
is necessary in order to remove calcium

126
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:33,480
to resorb bone.

127
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:35,300
The way I remember this is that

128
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:36,600
I happen to live in Chicago

129
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,200
and there is calcium in the water,

130
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:40,600
and so calcium can build up in the pipes

131
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:42,180
as they're used over time.

132
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:43,440
And so one of the recommendations

133
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:46,360
is to take a can of Coca-Cola or some kind of acid

134
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:48,540
and pour it down the pipes every six months.

135
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:49,480
And the idea is that

136
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,140
when you put that acid down the pipes,

137
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:53,840
the acid will leach the calcium from the pipes,

138
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,860
helping to remove calcium from the pipes.

139
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,960
So again, an acidic environment is necessary

140
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:01,420
in order to remove calcium from the bone

141
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,000
during the resorption process.

142
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:05,720
Now, in order to generate that acidic environment,

143
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:08,080
one of the enzymes that's particularly useful

144
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:09,480
is carbonic anhydrase.

145
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:12,280
Recall that carbonic anhydrase can take water

146
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:18,600
plus CO2 and combine it to produce H2CO3.

147
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:22,380
And of course, carbonic anhydrase catalyzes that reaction.

148
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:27,840
The H2CO3 then becomes acid plus bicarb,

149
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,520
and this acid will then go out of the cell

150
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:33,140
to create the acidic environment,

151
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:35,620
and the acidic environment will then result in

152
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:37,100
the resorption of bone.

153
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:40,140
Now, imagine if the osteoclasts don't have the ability

154
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:42,140
to generate this acidic environment,

155
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:44,100
then they won't be able to resorb bone.

156
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:45,820
And that's exactly what occurs

157
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:48,400
in the carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation.

158
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:50,680
And this becomes, again, very high yield

159
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:52,100
for the purposes of board exam.

160
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:54,120
Here's an x-ray of osteopetrosis,

161
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:55,220
and what should catch your attention

162
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:57,100
is that the bone is super thick.

163
00:05:57,420 --> 00:05:59,520
Now, remember, normally you should see a dark space here

164
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:00,760
where the medulla would be present,

165
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:04,100
but instead you just see a completely thickened bone.

166
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,940
And again, this image is also particularly high yield.

167
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:09,240
The clinical features of osteopetrosis

168
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:10,280
will be bone fracture.

169
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:12,480
Remember that the bone, although it's thick,

170
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:13,380
is going to be weak,

171
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:15,280
and that's because there is no proper balance

172
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:17,300
between the osteoclast and the osteoblast.

173
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:18,760
In order to make good, strong bone,

174
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:21,160
you need a balance between both the osteoclast

175
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:21,760
and the osteoblast.

176
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,340
The patients can also have anemia,

177
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:25,760
thrombocytopenia, and leukopenia.

178
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:28,580
Now, the idea here is that if this is bone,

179
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:30,780
you're getting a thickening of the bone,

180
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:33,620
which is beginning to replace the medullary space.

181
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:35,540
And when you replace the medullary space,

182
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,800
that's called a myelothysic process.

183
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:39,620
Now, this myelothysic process

184
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,380
is then going to knock out the ability

185
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:43,020
for hematopoiesis to occur,

186
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:44,700
and so the patients will get anemia,

187
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:46,820
thrombocytopenia, and leukopenia

188
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:48,900
with extra medullary hematopoiesis

189
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,120
in the spleen and the liver.

190
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,140
The patients can also develop vision

191
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:53,040
and hearing impairment,

192
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:54,580
and the idea here is that

193
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:56,500
you're going to get compression of cranial nerves

194
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,060
as they try to exit from the skull,

195
00:06:58,380 --> 00:07:00,120
again, because of the thickening of the bone.

196
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:01,900
There can also be hydrocephalus,

197
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,260
and that's because you can get thickening of the bone

198
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:05,840
at the foramen magnum.

199
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:07,820
A final feature is that the patients

200
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:09,760
can develop renal tubular acidosis.

201
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:11,420
This is when the patients develop

202
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:13,700
a metabolic acidosis within the blood

203
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:15,520
due to a defect in the tubule,

204
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:17,680
so we call that renal tubular acidosis.

205
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,720
And this is seen with carbonic anhydrase deficiency,

206
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:22,620
and is particularly high yield

207
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:24,480
because it ties back into physiology.

208
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,580
Recall that if this is the tubular cell,

209
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:30,460
and this is the lumen,

210
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:32,380
that the tubular cell has the ability

211
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:35,260
to take H2O and CO2

212
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:39,980
and combine it to H2CO3,

213
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:43,000
and of course it does that using carbonic anhydrase.

214
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,840
Now the H2CO3 will then break down

215
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:50,620
into hydrogen ion and HCO3, or bicarb,

216
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:53,780
and the hydrogen ion will be transferred to the lumen.

217
00:07:54,260 --> 00:08:00,760
It will join with NH3 to form NH4+.

218
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:04,480
NH4+, is important because this will then be excreted,

219
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:06,080
and so it's a way of getting rid of acid.

220
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:07,520
And when you get rid of this acid,

221
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:10,120
this will result in a net gain of bicarb

222
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:11,400
going into the blood,

223
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:13,020
maintaining the pH of the blood.

224
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:15,560
Now imagine if you don't have carbonic anhydrase,

225
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:17,480
you won't be able to generate this acid,

226
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:19,860
and therefore you won't be able to excrete this acid,

227
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:23,360
and therefore you won't be able to get this net gain of bicarb.

228
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:25,300
And if you don't get the net gain of bicarb,

229
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:27,120
the patients then will get acidotic.

230
00:08:27,420 --> 00:08:29,960
And so you'll get renal tubular acidosis.

231
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,700
And this again, this physiology,

232
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:33,400
is particularly high yield.

233
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:35,240
The treatment is bone marrow transplant,

234
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:37,420
and this is also particularly high yield.

235
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:38,400
You might think,

236
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,840
well why would a bone marrow transplant

237
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:41,320
help in osteopetrosis?

238
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:44,180
Well the answer is that remember that in osteopetrosis,

239
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:46,060
the problem is that the osteoclasts

240
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,160
are not functioning properly.

241
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:50,180
And remember that the osteoclasts are basically macrophages

242
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:51,440
that are present within the bone.

243
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:52,320
Now of course,

244
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:54,020
the osteoclasts or the macrophages

245
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:55,280
are derived from monocytes,

246
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:57,380
and the monocytes come from hematopoiesis.

247
00:08:57,780 --> 00:08:58,160
And therefore,

248
00:08:58,280 --> 00:08:59,440
if you give a bone marrow transplant,

249
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:00,640
you're going to give back the ability

250
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:01,880
to make normal monocytes,

251
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:03,660
and therefore you'll make normal osteoclasts.

252
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:04,740
And again,

253
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:06,980
this is a particularly high yield association

254
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,260
given all of that interplay.

255
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:11,920
The next two disorders are rickets and osteomalacia,

256
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:13,600
and in both of these disorders,

257
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,880
you have defective mineralization of osteoid.

258
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:17,780
Now let's go back and remind ourselves

259
00:09:17,780 --> 00:09:19,540
that the osteoblasts,

260
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:20,460
they lay down bone.

261
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:21,200
However,

262
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:22,400
they don't produce bone,

263
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:24,860
they actually produce a material called osteoid.

264
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:27,060
That osteoid then gets mineralized

265
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:28,280
by calcium and phosphate

266
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:29,900
to produce the final bone.

267
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,340
And so in rickets and osteomalacia,

268
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:35,180
this mineralization process is defective.

269
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:38,280
This is going to be due to low levels of vitamin D,

270
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,800
and recall that vitamin D acts on the intestine,

271
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:41,620
the kidney,

272
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:45,640
and the bone in order to resorb both calcium and phosphate.

273
00:09:46,100 --> 00:09:47,840
And so that's the primary role of vitamin D,

274
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,820
and that is to maintain both the calcium

275
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:51,660
and the phosphate within the blood.

276
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:54,080
Now some of the ways in which a vitamin D deficiency

277
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,820
could develop include decreased sun exposure.

278
00:09:57,200 --> 00:09:59,540
Recall from biochemistry that most of the vitamin D

279
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:01,160
in your blood is actually derived

280
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:03,860
from exposure of the skin to the sun.

281
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:06,400
It can also arise from poor diet.

282
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:08,680
There is a percentage of vitamin D

283
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:10,360
that comes from your diet.

284
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:12,320
It can arise from malabsorption.

285
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:14,700
Of course, vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin.

286
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:16,920
And then it can also arise from liver failure

287
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:17,880
and renal failure,

288
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:19,960
and that's because both the kidney and the liver

289
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:22,360
are essential for activating vitamin D.

290
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:25,200
When vitamin D deficiency arises in children,

291
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:26,200
we call that rickets,

292
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:29,220
and it usually arises in kids less than one year of age.

293
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:30,780
The classic clinical findings

294
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:32,420
are a pigeon breast deformity.

295
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:34,840
This is where you get an inward bending of the ribs

296
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,220
with an anterior protrusion of the sternum.

297
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:39,980
The patients can also develop frontal bossing.

298
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:43,180
This is when the forehead is particularly prominent,

299
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,740
and that arises due to osteoid deposition on the skull.

300
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:48,960
Another feature is rachytic rosary.

301
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:51,440
This is due to deposition of osteoid

302
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,260
at the costal-chondral junction.

303
00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:55,920
And so you can actually feel the costal-chondral junctions

304
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:56,700
in the patient's rib,

305
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:58,000
and they feel like little beads,

306
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:00,120
and so they call that a rachytic rosary,

307
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:02,780
the rosary being the beads that people worship on

308
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:04,780
and rachytic because you're dealing with rickets.

309
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:07,400
And finally, if the child is greater than the age of one

310
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:09,460
and is actually ambulating and walking around,

311
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:10,900
there can be bowing of the legs,

312
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:12,960
and that's because you get weakening of the long bones

313
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,980
as they grow from the growth plate.

314
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:17,160
Now, the central theme with all these

315
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:20,080
is that you're going to have abnormal mineralization of osteoid,

316
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,060
and often you'll see deposition of osteoid in various places.

317
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:27,520
For example, the rachytic rosary is due to deposition of osteoid

318
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,200
at the costal-chondral junction.

319
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:31,900
And I repeat this because board examiners like you to know

320
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:33,820
that there's deposition of osteoid.

321
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:36,240
If there's a deficiency in vitamin D in adults,

322
00:11:36,460 --> 00:11:38,080
we call that osteomalacia.

323
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:41,840
Now, this results in weak bone with an increased risk for fracture.

324
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:45,120
Now, the idea here is that your bone is always being turned over.

325
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,620
The osteoclasts are always removing bone,

326
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:50,420
and the osteoblasts are always laying down new bone.

327
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,460
And so imagine that if the osteoclasts are removing bone,

328
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:55,640
but the osteoblasts are laying down bone,

329
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:56,840
but it can't be mineralized,

330
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:00,220
then you're going to end up getting weak bone, which can fracture.

331
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:04,320
You'll have an increased risk of fracture in the weight-bearing areas,

332
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:07,960
for example, in the vertebral bodies or in the hip.

333
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:10,240
One of the key features of osteomalacia

334
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:12,540
is that the laboratories will be abnormal.

335
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:14,780
The calcium and the phosphate will be down

336
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:16,900
because there's a vitamin D deficiency.

337
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:20,600
And the PTH will be up because there's low calcium.

338
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,780
And finally, the alkaline phosphatase will be elevated.

339
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:25,920
I want to make sure that you understand

340
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:29,460
that whenever there is activation of the osteoblast,

341
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:31,900
there's going to be a high alkaline phosphatase.

342
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:34,200
And the idea here is that alkaline phosphatase

343
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:36,020
creates an alkaline environment,

344
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:38,480
and the alkaline environment is necessary

345
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:41,260
to lay down calcium into the osteoid.

346
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:43,640
Remember when we talked about osteopetrosis,

347
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,060
I told you that an acidic environment

348
00:12:45,060 --> 00:12:46,960
is necessary to remove calcium.

349
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:48,980
Well, the opposite is also true.

350
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:52,220
An alkaline environment is necessary to add calcium.

351
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:54,940
And so osteoblasts have alkaline phosphatase

352
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:57,360
because they create an alkaline environment

353
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:02,200
that will then allow them to calcify the unmineralized osteoid.

354
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:06,060
Osteoporosis is when you have a loss of trabecular bone mass,

355
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:09,940
and this results in porous bone, hence the term osteoporosis,

356
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,100
that has an increased risk for fracture.

357
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:15,680
The risk of osteoporosis is based on the peak bone mass

358
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:17,840
and the rate of bone loss thereafter.

359
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:19,520
In order to understand this,

360
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:21,160
you need to understand a basic principle.

361
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:24,080
Let's assume that this is bone mass on this axis here,

362
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:25,860
and this is age on this axis.

363
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,500
Then, over time, an individual develops something

364
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:30,760
called the peak bone mass.

365
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:33,760
This peak bone mass occurs at roughly the age of 30.

366
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:36,980
Now, there are three things that determine how high this peak will be.

367
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:38,760
Number one, your diet.

368
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:41,380
Number two, exercise.

369
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:45,560
And number three, the vitamin D receptor

370
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:47,900
that you inherit from your parents,

371
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:49,280
so your genetics, basically.

372
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:51,560
But in particular, the vitamin D receptor

373
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:52,920
that you inherit from your parents.

374
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:55,220
Now, these three factors play an important role

375
00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:58,200
in determining this height or this peak bone mass.

376
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:00,800
Now, once you peak at 30, it's all downhill,

377
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:04,120
and you begin to lose bone mass slowly over time.

378
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:05,360
There's no avoiding it,

379
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:08,160
and it's approximately less than 1% per year,

380
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:09,660
however it occurs in everyone.

381
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:12,160
Now, what determines the rate of this bone loss

382
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:16,080
is also going to be diet and exercise.

383
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:18,460
And so if there's a good diet and exercise,

384
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,060
you'll lose less than 1%.

385
00:14:20,060 --> 00:14:21,880
If the diet and exercise are poor,

386
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,380
you might lose bone at a more rapid rate.

387
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:28,840
And also, what's important is that estrogen is protective.

388
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:31,100
And so that when patients have estrogen on board,

389
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:32,880
they don't lose that much bone mass.

390
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:34,900
However, once estrogen is lost,

391
00:14:34,980 --> 00:14:36,580
for example, in a postmenopausal woman,

392
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:39,900
then you end up losing bone mass much more rapidly.

393
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:41,600
Now, let's remind ourselves that

394
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:43,820
when you cross this border, for example,

395
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:46,380
and you come underneath this border in bone mass,

396
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:48,520
the bone becomes weak and it fractures,

397
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:50,440
and that's called osteoporosis.

398
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:52,320
Now, with that background in mind,

399
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:53,660
let's go back and read the text.

400
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:55,640
Most common forms are senile.

401
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,000
Of course, if this is your peak bone mass,

402
00:14:58,080 --> 00:14:59,260
then the longer you live,

403
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:01,280
the more likely you are to cross that line.

404
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:03,280
And also, postmenopausal.

405
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:05,920
And again, that is because once you get peak bone mass,

406
00:15:06,380 --> 00:15:08,660
estrogen helps to prevent the loss of bone.

407
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:10,680
However, once estrogen is lost,

408
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:13,140
after menopause, bone is lost more rapidly.

409
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:15,280
And so you'll more quickly cross that line.

410
00:15:15,620 --> 00:15:18,440
So two most common forms, senile and postmenopausal.

411
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:21,080
Now, the clinical features are that you get bone pain

412
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:22,920
and fractures in weight-bearing areas.

413
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:25,940
And the weight-bearing areas include the vertebra,

414
00:15:26,600 --> 00:15:28,840
giving you loss of height and kyphosis,

415
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:31,240
the hip, giving you an increased risk of fracture,

416
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:32,480
and the distal radius,

417
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:34,400
also giving you an increased risk of fracture.

418
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:36,920
If you want to monitor the patient's bone density,

419
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:38,900
you can use something called a DEXA scan.

420
00:15:39,220 --> 00:15:41,260
And the idea here is that the DEXA scan

421
00:15:41,260 --> 00:15:43,820
allows you to determine where you are on this chart

422
00:15:43,820 --> 00:15:46,660
to determine how much bone mass you actually have.

423
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:50,260
And very high yield to note that in this particular disorder,

424
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:54,840
the calcium, phosphate, PTH, and alkaline phosphatase are all normal.

425
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:58,300
There will be no abnormal labs in this disorder.

426
00:15:58,620 --> 00:16:00,940
And that's very high yield because having normal labs

427
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:03,920
helps to distinguish this disease from osteomalacia,

428
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:05,960
which can present in a very similar way,

429
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:08,340
but would be due to a vitamin D deficiency.

430
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:12,160
Now, the treatment is exercise, vitamin D, and calcium.

431
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:15,080
And the goal here is to limit bone loss

432
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:19,700
so that you don't lose more than that approximately 1% per year.

433
00:16:20,620 --> 00:16:21,740
Bisphosphonates can be used.

434
00:16:22,220 --> 00:16:23,920
Bisphosphonates basically go into the blood,

435
00:16:24,220 --> 00:16:25,240
and they attach to the bone,

436
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:28,020
and then the osteoclasts eat the bisphosphonates,

437
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:30,820
and that induces apoptosis in the osteoclast.

438
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:33,460
Now, of course, if you remove the osteoclast from the picture,

439
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:35,840
then you won't be removing bone as much,

440
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:39,760
and so, therefore, you'll decrease the rate of loss of bone.

441
00:16:40,380 --> 00:16:42,640
Estrogen replacement therapy is debated.

442
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:44,900
However, theoretically, it would be helpful,

443
00:16:45,060 --> 00:16:47,580
and that's because estrogen protects against the loss of bone.

444
00:16:47,940 --> 00:16:49,920
And glucocorticoids are contraindicated,

445
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:53,340
and that's because they increase the risk of osteoporosis.

446
00:16:54,140 --> 00:16:55,940
Paget's disease of bone is an imbalance

447
00:16:55,940 --> 00:16:58,420
between osteoclast and osteoblast function.

448
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:01,340
Now, let's remind ourselves that the osteoblast lay down bone

449
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:03,060
and the osteoclast, they resorb bone.

450
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:05,480
And these two cells are in balance.

451
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:09,700
In fact, the osteoblast is the one that manages the osteoclast.

452
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:12,480
For example, when PTH is released by the parathyroid,

453
00:17:13,220 --> 00:17:17,280
it's very high yield to note that the PTH actually hits the osteoblast.

454
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:19,180
That's the cell that has the PTH receptor.

455
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:23,260
And the osteoblast then tells the osteoclast that it can resorb bone.

456
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:26,480
So the osteoclast does not work without the permission of the osteoblast.

457
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:29,380
Now, what happens in Paget's disease of bone

458
00:17:29,380 --> 00:17:31,360
is that the osteoclast goes crazy

459
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:34,860
and begins to resorb bone without the permission of the osteoblast.

460
00:17:34,860 --> 00:17:37,680
So he's resorbing bone, resorbing bone, resorbing bone,

461
00:17:37,740 --> 00:17:39,880
and the osteoblast is sort of just sitting there and watching.

462
00:17:40,420 --> 00:17:43,500
Now, once there's so much resorption of bone that the bone becomes weak,

463
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:46,660
the osteoblast then recognizes that it needs to lay down

464
00:17:46,660 --> 00:17:48,620
as much bone as possible to protect the bone.

465
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:50,660
So the osteoblast then joins in

466
00:17:50,660 --> 00:17:53,100
and starts trying to put down as much bone as possible,

467
00:17:53,240 --> 00:17:54,420
but it's doing it in a rush.

468
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:56,920
Finally, the osteoclast sort of burns out,

469
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,680
and then you're left with the osteoblast,

470
00:17:58,740 --> 00:18:01,660
and the osteoblast is just trying to lay down as much bone as possible,

471
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:05,460
but it's doing it in a rush and doesn't do a good job.

472
00:18:05,740 --> 00:18:07,620
And this is going to become more clear in a minute.

473
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:11,400
Now, this disorder is usually seen in late adulthood,

474
00:18:11,780 --> 00:18:13,300
approximately the age of 60.

475
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:16,360
And we don't know the etiology, but it's possibly viral.

476
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:20,340
So the idea here is that a virus would infect the osteoclast, making it crazy.

477
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:23,080
Important to note that this is a localized process

478
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:28,180
that involves one or more bones, it does not involve the entire skeleton.

479
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:30,440
Now, again, I've already told you about the stages.

480
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:32,720
In the first stage, the osteoclast goes crazy.

481
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:35,920
Eventually, the osteoblast needs to then lay down bone,

482
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:38,740
and so you get a mixed osteoblastic-osteoclastic phase

483
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:41,380
until finally the osteoclast sort of burns out,

484
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:45,240
and the osteoblast then begins to lay down as much bone as possible.

485
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:49,040
Now, the end result of all of this is that you get thick sclerotic bone

486
00:18:49,040 --> 00:18:52,100
because the osteoblast is laying down as much bone as possible,

487
00:18:52,100 --> 00:18:55,460
but it's not laid down with the balance of the osteoclast.

488
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:59,220
Remember that bone requires a proper balance, and so it fractures easily.

489
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:01,980
Here's an image of what happens in Paget's disease of bone,

490
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:04,180
and the idea here is that you get bone,

491
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:07,780
however, you have all of these cement lines in between the bone

492
00:19:07,780 --> 00:19:09,240
which have not been sealed.

493
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:11,360
And so although you have thick bone,

494
00:19:11,620 --> 00:19:14,920
the bone is very fragile because these lines have not yet been sealed.

495
00:19:15,260 --> 00:19:18,960
And so this is also called the mosaic pattern of lamellar bone.

496
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:22,320
Mosaic pattern because it appears that you have all these puzzle pieces.

497
00:19:22,420 --> 00:19:24,200
In fact, I call this puzzle piece bone.

498
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:28,740
And it's lamellar because you have all these lines of bone laid down appropriately.

499
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:30,660
Remember, bone is normally laid down in layers.

500
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:34,160
However, again, there's this mosaic puzzle piece pattern

501
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:37,320
because you haven't been able to properly connect the pieces of bone.

502
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:39,520
The clinical features are bone pain,

503
00:19:39,580 --> 00:19:41,640
and that's because you get microfractures of the bone.

504
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:45,580
One of the common clinical descriptions is that the patient comes in and says

505
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:49,980
they have an increasing hat size, and that's because the skull is commonly affected.

506
00:19:50,620 --> 00:19:53,520
And, of course, this disease results in thick sclerotic bone.

507
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:56,260
There can be hearing loss if you get thickening of the bone,

508
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:58,600
which then impringes on the cranial nerves.

509
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:00,560
There can be a lion-like facies,

510
00:20:00,620 --> 00:20:03,420
and that's when the facial bones become thick and sclerotic,

511
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:05,600
giving the face a lion-like appearance.

512
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:11,060
And very high yield, the patients often have an isolated, elevated alkaline phosphatase.

513
00:20:11,060 --> 00:20:13,760
Now, recall that at the end stage of this disorder,

514
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:16,340
the osteoblasts are going to be laying down bone.

515
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,980
And, of course, the osteoclasts have alkaline phosphatase

516
00:20:18,980 --> 00:20:22,460
to create an alkaline environment so that you can mineralize the bone.

517
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:24,980
And so you're going to have a high alkaline phosphatase,

518
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:26,320
and that's very high yield.

519
00:20:26,420 --> 00:20:27,420
The calcium will be normal.

520
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:28,600
The phosphate will be normal.

521
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:29,900
The PTH will be normal.

522
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:32,680
However, the alkaline phosphatase will be elevated.

523
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:34,800
The treatments include calcitonin.

524
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:37,720
Recall that calcitonin is sort of like the anti-PTH.

525
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:40,300
It does the opposite of what PTH would normally do.

526
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:43,940
And so if PTH normally causes the osteoclast to resorb bone,

527
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:48,000
then calcitonin actually inhibits the osteoclast from resorbing bone.

528
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:49,920
And so if you inhibit the osteoclast,

529
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:53,200
remember that the first phase of the disease was the osteoclastic phase.

530
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:57,100
You can then avoid the resorption of bone that occurs in this disorder.

531
00:20:57,500 --> 00:21:01,480
And bisphosphonates would result in apoptosis of the osteoclast,

532
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:04,820
of course, also then inhibiting the osteoclast in this disorder.

533
00:21:05,260 --> 00:21:07,260
The complications are particularly high yield

534
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:09,100
and include high output cardiac failure.

535
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:12,180
Now, the idea here is when you have all of this remodeling of bone,

536
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:14,340
you're going to get formation of AV shunts,

537
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:17,060
and the heart will then have to push through those AV shunts,

538
00:21:17,300 --> 00:21:19,780
which would then result in high output cardiac failure.

539
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,500
An additional complication is osteosarcoma.

540
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:26,140
Patients with Paget's disease of bone are at increased risk for osteosarcoma.

541
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:29,840
And the way I remember this is that the osteoblasts at the end of the disease

542
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:31,460
are going to be producing a ton of bone.

543
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:35,140
And if the osteoblasts get mutated in the process of this disorder,

544
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:37,180
they can then develop an osteosarcoma.

545
00:21:37,180 --> 00:21:40,980
And osteosarcoma is a malignant tumor of osteoblasts.

546
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:43,860
Osteomyelitis is infection of the marrow space in the bone,

547
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:45,500
and it usually occurs in children.

548
00:21:45,980 --> 00:21:47,460
It's most often bacterial,

549
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:52,180
and the bacteria get into the bone via hematogenous spread.

550
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:55,840
Now, in children, you get a transient bacteremia,

551
00:21:56,060 --> 00:21:59,100
which then results in seeding of the metaphysis,

552
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:02,000
whereas in adults, you classically have an open wound,

553
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:05,400
which results in bacteremia, which then seeds the epiphysis.

554
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:09,360
Recall that if this is bone, and this is the growth plate here,

555
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:13,540
you've got the epiphysis here, the metaphysis here, and the diaphysis here.

556
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:18,540
And so, again, in children, it's usually the metaphysis that gets seeded with bacteria,

557
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:21,220
whereas in adults, it's usually the epiphysis,

558
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:22,700
and that's actually high yield.

559
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:25,620
The causes of osteomyelitis include staph aureus.

560
00:22:25,680 --> 00:22:27,400
This is the overall most common cause.

561
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:32,160
Neisseria gonorrhea can do it, especially in sexually active young adults.

562
00:22:32,680 --> 00:22:35,820
Salmonella is the most common cause in sickle cell disease.

563
00:22:36,380 --> 00:22:39,980
Pseudomonas is an important cause in a diabetic or an IV drug abuser.

564
00:22:39,980 --> 00:22:45,980
Pasteurella is an important cause in a patient that has been exposed to a dog or cat bite

565
00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:47,500
or a dog or cat scratch.

566
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:50,580
And TB can also cause osteomyelitis.

567
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:52,440
And remember that when TB involves the bone,

568
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:56,260
it classically involves the lumbar vertebra, and we call that POTS disease.

569
00:22:56,600 --> 00:23:01,160
The clinical features of osteomyelitis is that you'll have bone pain with fever and leukocytosis.

570
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:03,340
Of course, the patient is infected with a bacteria.

571
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:07,720
The infection results in a lytic focus, which represents liquefactive necrosis,

572
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:10,260
which is surrounded by sclerosis on x-ray.

573
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:15,060
So the idea here is that if this is the bone, and of course in a child it would be in the metapasis,

574
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:20,820
you'll get an area of necrosis here, and then that will be surrounded by reactive bone,

575
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:24,220
which will then create sclerosis around that area of necrosis.

576
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:26,760
And so that's an important finding on x-ray.

577
00:23:27,020 --> 00:23:29,380
Then the diagnosis can be made by blood culture.

578
00:23:30,140 --> 00:23:33,960
The last disorder in this section is avascular aseptic necrosis.

579
00:23:33,960 --> 00:23:38,000
Now, again, here you get a focus of necrosis in the bone, but it's aseptic.

580
00:23:38,100 --> 00:23:41,360
It's not due to bacteria, as was the case in osteomyelitis.

581
00:23:41,780 --> 00:23:45,340
Instead, it's due to the fact that you have a lack of blood flow to the bone,

582
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:47,620
which then results in necrosis of the bone.

583
00:23:47,980 --> 00:23:51,120
So this is ischemic necrosis of the bone and the bone marrow.

584
00:23:51,580 --> 00:23:56,520
Causes include trauma or fracture, which can disrupt the blood supply to the bone, resulting in necrosis.

585
00:23:57,040 --> 00:23:59,700
Steroids increase the risk for avascular necrosis.

586
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:06,820
Sickle cell disease, the classic example being dactylitis, where you get vaso-occlusive crisis within the bones of the hands and the feet,

587
00:24:07,120 --> 00:24:10,480
resulting in avascular aseptic necrosis of the bone.

588
00:24:10,820 --> 00:24:16,460
NK sans disease, which is the circumstance in which you develop gas emboli, in particular nitrogen,

589
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:23,680
which then precipitates out of the blood and then can lodge within the bone, giving you an avascular necrosis.

590
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:28,540
Important to note that the complications of avascular necrosis include osteoarthritis.

591
00:24:28,540 --> 00:24:32,440
Of course, osteoarthritis is a wear and tear disease in which you damage the joint of the bone.

592
00:24:32,740 --> 00:24:39,660
If you have necrosis of the bone underneath the joint, that would increase the risk for damage to the joint, giving you osteoarthritis.

593
00:24:40,020 --> 00:24:43,560
And of course, if the bone is necrotic, it can also easily fracture.

594
00:24:43,860 --> 00:24:48,140
And so that would be a complication of avascular aseptic necrosis.

595
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:50,440
And that concludes this particular section.

